WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.580
A strawberry's red flesh is the mother's own tissue, with no chromosomes from the

2
00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:13.020
father. So why does it swell only after the flower is pollinated?

3
00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:16.510
The first film counted the sets in a seed.

4
00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:21.330
It ended with the seed coat and fruit wall as the mother's tissue.

5
00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:24.930
This film asks what tells her tissue to grow.

6
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.500
Start with the general rule.

7
00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:31.700
After fertilization, the ovule becomes the seed.

8
00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:36.580
In most flowering plants, the ovary around it becomes the fruit.

9
00:00:37.633 --> 00:00:39.693
A strawberry flower is unusual.

10
00:00:41.013 --> 00:00:43.653
It has many separate ovaries sitting on a dome.

11
00:00:44.853 --> 00:00:48.613
The dome is the receptacle, the swollen tip of the flower stalk.

12
00:00:49.683 --> 00:00:55.443
After fertilization, each ovary becomes an achene, a small dry fruit holding one

13
00:00:55.443 --> 00:00:59.383
seed. Those specks on the surface are the true fruits.

14
00:01:00.543 --> 00:01:02.763
The red flesh is swollen receptacle.

15
00:01:03.767 --> 00:01:08.647
A fruit whose flesh comes from tissue other than the ovary is called an accessory

16
00:01:08.647 --> 00:01:11.347
fruit. Compare a tomato.

17
00:01:12.707 --> 00:01:14.787
Its flesh develops from the ovary itself.

18
00:01:15.917 --> 00:01:20.517
In 1950, Jean Paul Nitsch asked what makes the receptacle grow.

19
00:01:21.797 --> 00:01:26.537
He worked on young receptacles from pollinated flowers, each studded with achenes.

20
00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:32.660
One idea is that the receptacle grows on its own once the flower is pollinated.

21
00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:35.840
If so, the achenes should not matter.

22
00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:40.380
So Nitsch removed every achene from some young receptacles.

23
00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:45.820
If pollination alone were enough, what would happen to a stripped receptacle?

24
00:01:52.283 --> 00:01:53.463
It did not enlarge.

25
00:01:54.823 --> 00:01:58.263
The intact receptacles grew, but the stripped ones stayed small.

26
00:01:59.803 --> 00:02:02.103
Growth needed the achenes, not just pollination.

27
00:02:03.317 --> 00:02:08.597
Next, Nitsch coated some stripped receptacles with a paste containing an auxin, a

28
00:02:08.597 --> 00:02:11.877
plant hormone. Those receptacles grew.

29
00:02:12.917 --> 00:02:14.677
Put the three results together.

30
00:02:15.937 --> 00:02:18.377
The developing achenes supply a growth signal.

31
00:02:19.997 --> 00:02:21.997
Applied auxin can stand in for that signal.

32
00:02:23.067 --> 00:02:24.967
Notice what this does not show.

33
00:02:26.507 --> 00:02:28.067
Auxin was enough to restore growth.

34
00:02:29.187 --> 00:02:32.287
That does not prove the natural signal is auxin alone.

35
00:02:33.587 --> 00:02:35.447
Nor does it show how the signal moves.

36
00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:37.640
Back to the ledger.

37
00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:41.360
The flesh is all mother, two magenta blocks.

38
00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:46.620
In a normal fruit, it grows on a signal from the developing achenes.

39
00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:51.130
This is one experiment, in one kind of accessory fruit.

40
00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:54.370
The receptacle is the mother's tissue.

41
00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:57.630
But the developing achenes tell it to grow.
